<%# Design preview: remove the trading floor's Change column, and mark a price that is not today's.
    Development only.

    This is the third proposal about the same figure - a scrolling ticker, then a movers card, then this
    column - and the honest reading of what the last two rounds turned up is that the *daily change* is not
    worth a column, while the **age of the price** is worth stating. That is the reversal this page argues
    for, and if the answer is "stop changing it", leaving the column as it is costs nothing. %>
<% content_for :own_heading, true %>

<% today = Date.current %>
<% rows = [
     { company: "The Coca-Cola Company", ticker: "KO", price: 68.99, priced: today,
       note: "Priced today - nothing extra to say" },
     { company: "Carnival Corporation", ticker: "CCL", price: 21.85, priced: today - 4,
       note: "The fetch has failed for four days - this is what a student would buy at" },
     { company: "Newly Added Co.", ticker: "NEW", price: 12.50, priced: nil,
       note: "Never successfully priced" }
   ] %>

<%= render layout: "components/ui/page_header",
           locals: { title: "Price change, reconsidered",
                     description: "Why the daily change is not worth a column, and what is" } do %><% end %>

<%= render "components/ui/callout",
           tone: :warning,
           title: "This is a proposal to remove something shipped two commits ago",
           body: "Development only, nothing is wired up, and the Change column is still live on the " \
                 "trading floor. Say the word and it stays exactly as it is - the case below is a case, " \
                 "not a fait accompli." %>

<div class="mt-6 space-y-10">
  <%# ------------------------------------------------------------------ the case %>
  <section aria-labelledby="case">
    <div class="mb-3">
      <h2 id="case" class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900">Four facts about the daily change</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="tw-card p-5">
      <dl class="space-y-4 text-sm">
        <div>
          <dt class="font-medium text-slate-900">"Since yesterday" is wrong two days a week</dt>
          <dd class="mt-1 max-w-2xl text-slate-600">
            The job runs on the cron <code class="text-xs">0 2 * * 2-6</code> - Tuesday to Saturday. Nothing
            runs for Sunday or Monday, so on a Monday the figure spans Friday to Saturday and still says
            yesterday. Market holidays widen the same gap.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div>
          <dt class="font-medium text-slate-900">Without an API key it is an em dash, always</dt>
          <dd class="mt-1 max-w-2xl text-slate-600">
            <code class="text-xs">ALPHA_VANTAGE_API_KEY</code> is not set in this environment, and
            <code class="text-xs">fetch_quote</code> returns nil without one - so the column is empty by
            default in any environment that has not been given a key.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div>
          <dt class="font-medium text-slate-900">Rate limits make a partial update the normal case</dt>
          <dd class="mt-1 max-w-2xl text-slate-600">
            18 stocks, one request each, with a 1.1s sleep between them for the free tier's limit. When the
            allowance runs out mid-run, some companies are fresh and some are days old - and the column
            renders both identically, which is the failure that was just fixed in the job.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div>
          <dt class="font-medium text-slate-900">It is hidden from the audience</dt>
          <dd class="mt-1 max-w-2xl text-slate-600">
            <code class="text-xs">hidden lg:table-cell</code>, because a third always-visible column puts
            that table into a sideways scroll at 375px. These students are largely on phones, so the column
            is absent exactly where they are.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-sm text-slate-600">
      And the change figure that <em>is</em> worth having already exists, on the portfolio: current value
      minus what was paid, per holding. It needs no API for its baseline - the purchase price is stored - so
      it is always computable and always true. Note the collision that has been sitting there: "Change"
      means <strong class="font-medium text-slate-900">since purchase</strong> on one page and
      <strong class="font-medium text-slate-900">since yesterday</strong> on the other.
    </p>
  </section>

  <%# ------------------------------------------------------------------ the proposal %>
  <section aria-labelledby="proposal">
    <div class="mb-3">
      <h2 id="proposal" class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900">
        Instead: say how old the price is
        <span class="ml-1 align-middle"><%= render "components/ui/badge", label: "Recommended", tone: :success %></span>
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-1 max-w-2xl text-sm text-slate-600">
        Whatever else is true, a student places an order against this number. If it is four days old,
        nothing on the page currently says so - and that is worth more than a percentage, because it bears
        on the transaction rather than on the browsing.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="table-container">
      <div class="table-wrapper overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="table-base table-fixed">
          <thead>
            <tr class="table-header-row">
              <th scope="col" class="table-header-cell table-header-cell-left">Company (exchange)</th>
              <th scope="col" class="table-header-cell table-header-cell-right w-40">Last price</th>
              <th scope="col" class="table-header-cell table-header-cell-left w-32">Your holdings</th>
              <th scope="col" class="table-header-cell table-header-cell-right w-52">
                <span class="sr-only">Actions</span>
              </th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="bg-white">
            <% rows.each do |row| %>
              <tr class="table-body-row">
                <td class="table-body-cell">
                  <span class="block font-medium text-slate-900"><%= row[:ticker] %></span>
                  <span class="block text-sm text-slate-600"><%= row[:company] %></span>
                </td>

                <%# The date only when it is not today's, so a fresh price stays a clean figure and the note
                    means something when it appears. slate-600 at 7.58:1, not amber or rose: a stale price is
                    a fact about the data, not an error the student has made. %>
                <td class="table-body-cell table-body-cell-right">
                  <span class="block tabular-nums text-slate-900"><%= number_to_currency(row[:price]) %></span>
                  <% if row[:priced].nil? %>
                    <span class="mt-0.5 block text-xs text-slate-600">Not priced yet</span>
                  <% elsif row[:priced] != today %>
                    <span class="mt-0.5 block text-xs text-slate-600">
                      as of <%= row[:priced].strftime("%-d %b") %>
                    </span>
                  <% end %>
                </td>

                <td class="table-body-cell"><span class="table-cell-content">You own 0</span></td>
                <td class="table-body-cell table-body-cell-right">
                  <div class="flex justify-end gap-2">
                    <span class="tw-btn-secondary">Buy</span>
                    <span class="tw-btn-secondary">Sell</span>
                  </div>
                </td>
              </tr>
            <% end %>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>

    <%= render layout: "components/ui/card", locals: { padded: false } do %>
      <ul class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
        <% rows.each do |row| %>
          <li class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline justify-between gap-4 px-5 py-3">
            <span class="text-sm text-slate-600"><%= row[:note] %></span>
            <span class="text-sm font-medium text-slate-900">
              <%= number_to_currency(row[:price]) %><% if row[:priced].nil? %> &middot; not priced yet<% elsif row[:priced] != today %> &middot; as of <%= row[:priced].strftime("%-d %b") %><% end %>
            </span>
          </li>
        <% end %>
      </ul>
    <% end %>

    <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-sm text-slate-600">
      It is visible at every width, because it lives in a column that already exists rather than adding one -
      which is the other reason to prefer it over the change figure.
    </p>
  </section>

  <%# ------------------------------------------------------------------ what it costs %>
  <section class="tw-card p-5" aria-labelledby="cost">
    <h2 id="cost" class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900">What it costs, and the honest alternative</h2>

    <dl class="mt-4 space-y-4 text-sm">
      <div>
        <dt class="font-medium text-slate-900">Doing it</dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 max-w-2xl text-slate-600">
          Remove the Change column from the header, the row and the below-lg block, take the column count in
          the empty row's colspan back to four, widen Last price to <code class="text-xs">w-40</code> for the
          second line, and add the date. `movement_class` stays - the portfolio still uses it. Two of the six
          tests in `price_change_test` change, and `migration.md` gets the removal. About an hour.
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div>
        <dt class="font-medium text-slate-900">The argument against, which is real</dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 max-w-2xl text-slate-600">
          This would be the third revision of one small feature, and churn has a cost of its own. The column
          is not harmful now that the job no longer fabricates zeros: it is honest, it just says
          <em>nothing</em> most of the time. Leaving it and only adding the date is a coherent middle - you
          keep a figure that is right when it is there, and gain the staleness marker that matters.
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div>
        <dt class="font-medium text-slate-900">What it needs from you</dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 max-w-2xl text-slate-600">
          One of three: remove the column and add the date; keep the column and add the date; or stop here,
          because the job fix already removed the part that was actively misleading.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>
</div>
